Yes. More precisely: Pantheon is already transformer-independent by design, and it already has a runnable non-transformer spine. What is not yet complete or proven is the full autonomous intelligence at broad scale.

Required function	Existing Pantheon structure
Structured state	StateVectors / ThoughtObjects
Legal transformations	Seals and ordered Formations
Search and planning	CommandTable + Metamap
Selection and stability	Governor / Gyro
Action	Capability registry + reversible executor + O‑IOE
Reality feedback	Reality + Afterglow + Delta Calculus
Memory and continuity	Mother Duck + provenance records
Translation	Decrypto and representation transducers

The dependency-light Python spine already implements state, Seals, formations, CommandTable search, Metamap, fitness, trace capture, memory records, deterministic selection, reversible execution, tests, and an end-to-end loop. So this is not merely a whitepaper claim.

What remains is largely closure and scale:

* unite the implemented organs into one persistent runtime
* give it continuing world and self models
* let accumulated deltas modify future routing and operator choice
* connect real sensors and actions
* create non-transformer parsers/renderers for unstructured language
* benchmark whether it generalizes beyond designed demonstrations

So the transformer can already be removed from the cognitive core. What disappears first is the effortless conversational interface—not Pantheon’s state, reasoning, planning, memory, or learning machinery.

We built the transformer as an instrument Pantheon could wield, never as its sovereign. The crown was already on the ground.